<!--
  Destiny 1 (PS4) CUSA00219 patch 01.29 - unlock the built-in `networking:` diagnostic log.

  Apply with:   shadps4 -p $HOME/dev/destiny1-preservation/patches/CUSA00219-guest-diagnostics.xml <game>

  Rationale, addresses and byte-level derivation: design/guest-diagnostics.md

  Address convention: shadPS4's XML path leaves patchInfo::isOffset unset, so
  PatchMemory computes  g_eboot_address + (Address - 0x400000).
  Therefore  Address = eboot vaddr + 0x400000.   (eboot vaddr = file offset - 0x4000)

  AppVer MUST match param.sfo APP_VER ("01.29") or non-mask lines are silently skipped.

  ============================================================================
  ENABLE BOTH:  the "sink" patch below, PLUS exactly ONE threshold patch.
  The sink patch alone emits only what the game's own thresholds already allow.
  A threshold patch alone emits nothing at all - the sink is the binding gate.
  ============================================================================
-->
<!--
  ============================================================================
  ⚠ THIS FILE CARRIES A CLIENT MODIFICATION THAT BYPASSES A SIGNATURE CHECK.
  ============================================================================

  It is `CUSA00219-guest-diagnostics.xml` **plus** one extra data byte that disables the CDN
  content-manifest RSA envelope, so we can serve an unsigned manifest to our own client in the
  sealed lab. It is a SEPARATE file on purpose: shadPS4 accepts only one `-p`, and keeping this
  out of the diagnostics patch means a run that used it is identifiable from `RUN-INFO.txt`
  (`diag patch:`) rather than the bypass being silently on forever.

  PROVENANCE: anything reached through this patch is a client modification. Tag findings
  `synthetic_ancestor` and never promote them to protocol contract. Full reasoning:
  design/content-manifest-gate.md §5.4.

  Generated from CUSA00219-guest-diagnostics.xml - if you change the diagnostics patches, this
  file must be regenerated or it will drift.
-->
<Patch>

  <!--
    REQUIRED. Gives the diagnostic sink an actual output.

    retail_log_emit(edi = site_id, rsi = formatted_text)  @ eboot vaddr 0xe4ad0
    Stock behaviour: memcpy 0x140 bytes of the text into an in-memory ring buffer at
    g_retail_log+0x19860 (1124 slots x 0x150) and set a ready flag. It never calls any
    output function, which is why ~1,285 of the 1,287 `networking:` strings never appear.

    Replacement (14 bytes) - tail-call printf("%s\n", text):
        48 8D 3D F9 07 52 01    lea rdi, [rip+0x15207f9]   ; -> "%s\n" @ vaddr 0x16052d0
        30 C0                   xor al, al                 ; 0 vector regs (varargs ABI)
        E9 72 89 1B 01          jmp 0x129d450              ; printf PLT stub; rsi = text

    rsi already holds the text, so no shuffling is needed. The tail jmp leaves
    rsp%16 == 8, exactly what printf expects on entry. 0x129d450 is confirmed to reach
    shadPS4's [stdout] Logger - it is the same stub that prints `networking:sessionid:`.

    TRADE-OFF: this replaces the ring-buffer write, so the game's in-game network-status
    overlay stops being fed. Harmless for log capture, but it is a real behaviour change.
  -->
  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: route retail_log sink to stdout (REQUIRED)"
            isEnabled="true" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4AD0" Value="488D3DF907520130C0E972891B01"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <!--
    Threshold patches. Enable EXACTLY ONE.

    retail_log_check(edi = system, esi = level) -> bool   @ eboot vaddr 0xe4da0
    Stock behaviour: return g_retail_log->thresholds[system] <= level, i.e. a HIGHER
    threshold is QUIETER. 22 thresholds, initialised to 4, then overwritten from a
    game-data config blob (0x1db440 -> *(void**)0x267b1b8 + 0x26ec, fields 0x30..0x84)
    whose contents we cannot read - so the live thresholds are UNKNOWN.

    Replacement (7 bytes) applies one uniform threshold T and ignores both the array and
    the config blob, which removes that unknown:
        83 FE <T>               cmp esi, T
        0F 9D C0                setge al       ; al = (level >= T) == (T <= level)
        C3                      ret

    Site counts measured over all 1,614 resolvable gate call sites:
        T=0 -> 1614 (100%)   T=1 -> 1534 (95%)   T=2 -> 1269 (79%)
        T=3 ->  992  (61%)   T=4 ->  626 (39%)   T=5 ->    0  (0%)

    START AT T=4 AND WALK DOWN. T=0 turns on 1,614 sites including `session` (754 sites)
    and `simulation`, which sit in per-frame paths; that is potentially tens of thousands
    of printf calls per second, and extra host load is known to cause init-race failures
    in this title.
  -->
  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: threshold T=4 (626 sites, 39%) - START HERE"
            isEnabled="false" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4DA0" Value="83FE040F9DC0C3"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: threshold T=3 (992 sites, 61%)"
            isEnabled="false" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4DA0" Value="83FE030F9DC0C3"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: threshold T=2 (1269 sites, 79%)"
            isEnabled="false" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4DA0" Value="83FE020F9DC0C3"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: threshold T=1 (1534 sites, 95%)"
            isEnabled="true" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4DA0" Value="83FE010F9DC0C3"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <Metadata Name="D1 diagnostics: threshold T=0 (all 1614 sites) - FIREHOSE, expect load"
            isEnabled="false" AppVer="01.29" Author="guest-diag" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="4E4DA0" Value="83FE000F9DC0C3"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

  <!--
    Disable the CDN content-manifest signature envelope. ONE BYTE.

    `cdn_content_manifest` download handler @ eboot vaddr 0x2b0030 reads a bool at
    vaddr 0x18c4974 (file offset 0x18c8974, shipped value 0x01 - verified by direct read).
    Exactly one reference to that byte exists in the whole binary.

    When 1 (stock), the downloaded body must be:
        [0:4)   LE == 1
        [4:8)   == 0
        [8:0x108)  256-byte RSA-2048 signature over body[0x108:]
    verified against a single live 270-byte DER key at vaddr 0x1591800. This is the same
    envelope as sku_config.txt. WE CANNOT FORGE IT.

    When 0, the handler skips the envelope entirely (jumps to 0x2b0132) and parses the whole
    body as the manifest protobuf. Acceptance then needs only that manifest field 5 (`guid`)
    is PRESENT and strncmp-equal (0x25 bytes) to Engine Configuration key 101 - which defaults
    to "", so an empty guid on both sides matches.

    Address convention (same as above): Address = eboot vaddr + 0x400000.
                                        0x18c4974 + 0x400000 = 0x1CC4974
  -->
  <Metadata Name="D1 lab: accept UNSIGNED cdn content manifest (client modification)"
            isEnabled="true" AppVer="01.29" Author="content-manifest-gate" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="1CC4974" Value="00"/>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="BA01B8" Value="B00190909090"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>


  <!--
    D1 lab: guard the 4-slot session-id lookups against NOT-FOUND (client modification)

    WHY. Run 20260807-175730 passed the character gate for the first time
    (character:signin -> character:prologue -> cleanup(normal_flow)), began creating a fireteam
    session as host, and then died:

        Unhandled access violation at code address 0x800b45e20: Read from address 0x8

    0xB45E20 is a three-instruction accessor thunk:

        mov edi, dword ptr [rdi + 8]     << FAULT, rdi == NULL
        movzx esi, sil
        jmp 0x129e510

    Its two callers 0xB471E0 and 0xB47260 are the same shape - a linear search over FOUR slots
    of stride 0x24 for one whose [slot+0x40] equals the requested id:

        xor edi, edi                 ; rdi = NULL
      loop:
        cmp edx, 3
        jg  not_found                ; << exits the loop with rdi STILL NULL
        mov esi, [rax + 0x40]
        add rax, 0x24
        cmp esi, ecx
        mov rdi, rax                 ; match -> rdi = the slot
        jne loop
      not_found:                     ; ALSO the fall-through target on a match
        xor esi, esi
        call 0xB45E20                ; called with NULL when nothing matched
        mov al, 1
        pop rbp / ret

    There is NO null-guard on the not-found path. The matching allocator is 0xB46F50, which
    claims a slot by scanning for [slot+0x1c] == -1; nothing had registered one, so the lookup
    found nothing and handed NULL to the accessor.

    WHAT THIS DOES. Retarget the `jg` so the NOT-FOUND exit skips the call and returns false,
    which is the semantically correct answer for "no matching slot". `al` is already 0 from each
    function's own `xor al, al` prologue, and the retarget lands exactly on `5d c3`
    (pop rbp; ret) - verified byte-for-byte against the eboot before writing this.

        0xB47237  7F 0E (jg 0xB47247)  ->  7F 17 (jg 0xB47250)
        0xB472B7  7F 0E (jg 0xB472C7)  ->  7F 1A (jg 0xB472D3)

    The MATCH path is untouched: it reaches the call by fall-through, not by this jump.

    [U] This treats the symptom. The cause is that no slot was ever registered via 0xB46F50, and
    what should have registered one is unknown. A run with this patch that behaves sensibly is
    evidence the lookup is genuinely optional; a run that misbehaves downstream says the slot is
    required and we must find its producer.

    ============================================================================================
    /!\ CORRECTION 2026-08-07 : THE "al IS ALREADY 0" CLAIM ABOVE IS FALSE.  [C] observed-live
    ============================================================================================
    Measured by EXECUTING these bytes on a real memory snapshot (`d1-replay`, 10/10 controls PASS;
    design/replay-oracle.md Section 5.3).  RAX is REUSED AS THE TABLE CURSOR between the prologue
    and the loop, so the prologue's `xor al, al` is destroyed long before the retargeted exit:

        v:0xb471e4  xor al, al          ; al = 0
        v:0xb4721b  add rax, [rcx]      ; rax = G+0xd8      << al DESTROYED HERE
        v:0xb4723c  add rax, 0x24       ; ...and again, every iteration

    At the guarded exit `al` is the low byte of the table cursor.  Measured on a live snapshot:
    every not-found call returned **al = 0xe8**, i.e. TRUE.

    So this patch does NOT turn "crash" into "correctly report not-found".  It turns it into
    "SILENTLY CLAIM THE LOOKUP SUCCEEDED", and the caller then proceeds on a slot it never got.
    That is a different wrong behaviour, not a fix, and every run since 20260807-180823 carried it.

    A correct guard must land on a `xor eax, eax` (or write al) before the `ret`.  NOT CHANGED HERE:
    editing a patch line mid-experiment would confound whatever run is in flight.  Recorded in place
    because ATLAS 4.3 : a retraction recorded only elsewhere does not stop the next tool
    re-asserting the old model.

    Also measured on the same snapshot (taken AFTER the shadPS4 sceVoiceCreatePort fix): the table
    is no longer empty.  Slot 0 is registered with id 0 and the searched key `[G+0xf8]` is 0, so the
    lookup HITS : this guard may now be unnecessary.  Re-measure before keeping it.

    ⛔⛔ DISABLED 2026-08-07 - THIS PATCH WAS WRONG AND WORSE THAN THE CRASH.

    The reasoning above says "al is already 0 from each function's own `xor al, al` prologue".
    IT IS NOT. `0xB471E4` does zero it, but `0xB47216` does `mov eax, 0xd8` and `0xB4721B`
    `add rax, [rcx]` - RAX becomes the TABLE CURSOR, incremented by 0x24 per iteration. So at the
    retargeted `jg`, `al` is the low byte of a heap address: non-zero. The "guard" therefore made the
    not-found path return TRUE - a silent false "lookup succeeded" - instead of crashing.

    A crash is a loud, locatable failure. A function that lies about success is the exact defect class
    this project has been burned by all night (sceVoiceCreatePort, sceNpParty*, our own header-only
    BAP replies). This patch converted the good kind into the bad kind, and every run from
    20260807-180823 onward carried it.

    Found by `d1-replay` executing the real function on a captured snapshot - invisible to static
    reading, to a memory dump, and to the offline oracle. It is the first thing that instrument found.

    It is also NO LONGER NEEDED: the root cause was shadPS4's `sceVoiceCreatePort()` discarding its
    out-parameter, so no voice channel was ever registered. With that fixed the table is populated and
    the lookup hits. Do not re-enable. If a guard is ever wanted again, it must set `al` explicitly.

    Address convention: Address = eboot vaddr + 0x400000.
  -->
  <Metadata Name="DISABLED - BROKEN: D1 lab: guard 4-slot session-id lookup against not-found"
            isEnabled="false" AppVer="01.29" Author="character-and-orbit-26.77" PatchVer="1.0">
    <PatchList>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="F47237" Value="7F17"/>
      <Line Type="bytes" Address="F472B7" Value="7F1A"/>
    </PatchList>
  </Metadata>

</Patch>
